\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 Elsie was never fond of regular paintings. She is, however, very fond of limnings\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 paintings that have come alive. As a custodian in a gallery for limnings, she watches over fantastical creatures and sentient portraits. One of them, especially, has captured Elsie\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s interest and maybe her heart: Theodore Quill, an enigmatic aristocrat who hides within his paintings from all but Elsie.\line When robbers raid the gallery, Elsie can\u8217\'92t bear the thought of Theo\u8217\'92s portrait being stolen, so she does something with questionable legality. Something only a limner\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 a painter of magical paintings\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 can do. Elsie reaches into the painting and frees him.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 But Theo is no painting, and he never was. Over a century ago, he became trapped within a limning, rendering him a mere spectator of the real world. Now stranded in 1899, Theo has no home to return to, and Elsie welcomes him into the house she shares with her grandmother, hiding Theo\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s true origin. Even from herself. For the truth would make her the one thing her grandmother despises: a limner.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 Lies about Theo\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s background and Elsie\u8217\'92s newfound ability pile up until the truth ultimately spills out. Feeling shunned by her only family, Elsie joins Theo on his search for remnants of his past. But she discovers more than she bargained for. The corrupt gallery owner has taken an interest in Elsie's ability; Theo\u8217\'92s entrapment wasn't entirely accidental, and he\u8217\'92s keeping a fatal secret. Theo is dying. Elsie must race against the clock\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 evading the gallery owner's notice\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 to save the man she's fallen for or lose him forever.}
